From 72abb31ff3e9924c01e32a0dd4f5143fc7519e21 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 3 Nov 2026 00:26:32 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] fix(copy-drafts): budget verification from what every
 rank holds alike

Base: candidate-37's patch head 5b7cfa37f (tree af0fb152), the series of
experiments/2026-10-26-spark-ds41f-candidate/candidate-37/vllm/series.

Rationale (#852): the adaptive budget capped each request's scheduled drafts
with CopyDrafter's host mirror of the per-slot draft limits. Patch 0612 made
that mirror non-blocking: sync_slot_draft_limits publishes the latest
proposal's limits only once their copy has landed, and otherwise keeps an
older proposal's. Each rank reads its own mirror at its own moment, and nothing
makes the ranks agree on it. So the budget (the step's token count, its drafts
per request and the device layout made from them) could depend on the rank and
on when a copy landed. The budget's other inputs are rank-wide: the
scheduler's draft counts, and the confidence snapshot, which
record_confidences waits for and broadcasts from rank 0 (or which is
rank-invariant by construction). The runner now gives the manager no host
limits (slot_draft_limits=None), so every rank lays out the same step, and no
host waits.

Quality: placeholder drafts (-1 past a proposal's drafts), their rejection,
sampling, dead rows and the copy machinery are candidate-37's. The budget may
now verify more rows than the latest proposal has. When the proposal narrowed
below the rows the scheduler laid out (a rejected or lost copy), the step
verifies its -1 placeholders where the planned confidences pay for them.
Verification rejects them, so the committed tokens are the target's.
- With dead rows (SPARK_DS41F_DSPARK_DEAD_ROWS_TAU above 0, the recipe's 0.2)
  they have confidence 0 and are padding, which the router skips.
- Without dead rows they are routed live, more often than candidate-37 routed
  them (there only on a stale read).
A stale narrow limit no longer drops drafts. Where candidate-37's host limit
did not bind (it was current and at least the scheduled drafts), every step is
bit for bit candidate-37's. Where it bound, the step has more rows and the
same temperature-0 text. DSpark's confidences come from the checkpoint's head,
not an online estimator, so the extra rows teach the budget nothing, and the
copy state counts at most a copy's own drafts (0612's min with pending).

Credit: Jordi Posthumus (#852, #855) diagnosed the cause: 0612's non-blocking
limit read lets the budget act on an older proposal's limits.

Tests (tests/v1/spec_decode/test_copy_drafts.py):
- test_ranks_budget_alike_whenever_their_limit_copies_land: two ranks with the
  same histories, proposals and confidence snapshot, one's limit copy landed
  and the other's in flight. Without host limits they budget the same step
  and device layout; with them (candidate-37's wiring) they split.
- test_verification_through_copy_growth_shrinkage_and_slot_reuse: two
  requests in one slot, decoded through the copy drafter, the budget, the
  draft combine and greedy verification. Copies grow past the initial cap; a
  rejected copy's next proposal is narrower than its step, whose -1
  placeholders are verified and rejected; the committed text is the target's
  greedy text.
- 0612's test_stale_wide_allocation_rejects_placeholder_tail_and_doubles_cap_
  without_sync is unchanged.
In candidate-37's image, test_copy_drafts.py, test_copy_drafts_scheduler.py,
test_adaptive_verification.py and test_prepare_kernels.py go from 77 to 79
passed, with 1 skipped (the two-GPU test).

Upstream status: local (spark-ds41f). Copy drafts and adaptive verification's
budget are local patches.
